# Targets chezmoi should remove from the destination directory on apply, one per line,
# relative to $HOME (the destination dir) -- chezmoi's own PatternSet format, the same one
# .chezmoiignore uses. Dropping a file from the source tree does NOT delete its deployed
# copy; chezmoi's own remove-on-apply mechanism (this file) is what makes the diff actually
# stick. Without an entry here, a retired file survives `chezmoi apply` as an unmanaged
# leftover: slice 3 found this the hard way -- a rc=0 apply left all six of its deleted
# allow hooks executable in ~/.claude/hooks/, discovered only by driving them by hand.

# claude-guard slice 3 cutover (docs/specs/2026-09-06-claude-guard-design.md, Rollout row 3):
# the six bash PermissionRequest hooks it replaced, plus the allow-daniel-server test oracle
# that had no chezmoi-managed sibling once its source (executable_allow-daniel-server.sh) was
# deleted.
.claude/hooks/allow-compound-bash.sh
.claude/hooks/allow-readonly-remote.sh
.claude/hooks/allow-safe-curl.sh
.claude/hooks/allow-safe-rm.sh
.claude/hooks/allow-ansible-readonly.sh
.claude/hooks/allow-daniel-server.sh
.claude/hooks/test_allow_daniel_server.py

# claude-guard slice 6 (docs/specs/2026-09-06-claude-guard-design.md, Rollout row 6): the
# PreToolUse deny hook and the segmenter it sourced. Slice 4 unregistered the hook on the host
# but kept the file, because the sandbox bind-mounted the deployed copy as its own in-container
# deny hook; the sandbox port (dotfiles #508) repointed that mount and registration at
# guard-pre-tool-use.sh, so nothing runs either file any more.
.claude/hooks/block-dangerous-bash.sh
.claude/hooks/cmdparse.sh

# #538: the agent-view chain (agentview itself was removed in 0e6bded; the three hooks were
# re-added in 8d642ae for a sandbox caller that 0e6bded had already deleted, so nothing
